1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
Does deporting migrants reduce crime?

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:07,160
Welcome to this new in-depth analysis, where we will explore one of the most heated

3
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:12,160
and debated issues of all time, and we will do it in a slightly different way than usual, completely putting

4
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:17,359
aside personal opinions and politics to rely exclusively on pure economic data.

5
00:00:17,359 --> 00:00:22,760
We will analyze together a truly fascinating paper by economist Paolo Pinotti, titled "Immigration

6
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:25,960
Enforcement and Crime," published in the American Economic Review.

7
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:31,280
The goal here is to answer an extremely complex question in an entirely objective way.

8
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:36,719
Do stricter deportation policies really have a real impact on crime rates?

9
00:00:36,719 --> 00:00:38,399
Let's dive right in.

10
00:00:38,399 --> 00:00:42,200
Today we will structure our investigation into six quick steps.

11
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:47,240
We will start by trying to understand what the deportation dilemma is, and then delve into the

12
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:50,359
Italian quota system and the role of international agreements.

13
00:00:50,359 --> 00:00:54,240
After that, we will look at the ingenious experiment of the so-called "click days,"

14
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:59,719
we will analyze the real data on crime, and finally, we will draw conclusions for policy.

15
00:00:59,719 --> 00:01:02,880
Point number one: the deportation dilemma.

16
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:06,920
In short, what is the mystery that economists find themselves facing?

17
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:12,120
To untangle this knot, we must familiarize ourselves with a key concept in economics:

18
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:14,079
opportunity cost.

19
00:01:14,079 --> 00:01:17,760
Imagine the situation of someone who finds themselves in a country without documents:

20
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:20,120
being unable to work in the official sector,

21
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:23,840
the opportunities to generate a legal income drop drastically.

22
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:27,600
Consequently, the cost of risking a criminal activity, calculated

23
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:31,040
in terms of legal job opportunities lost in the event of arrest,

24
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:34,920
is much, much lower compared to those who have regular status.

25
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:39,040
And, as the data confirms, limited access to the official economy

26
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:41,840
inevitably increases the appeal of crime.

27
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:43,840
And here, the dilemma arises.

28
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:48,000
What really happens if a country tightens deportations?

29
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,600
On one hand, we have the so-called incapacitation effect.

30
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:55,680
If you physically deport a person, you obviously reduce to zero their possibility

31
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:58,160
of committing crimes in that territory.

32
00:01:58,160 --> 00:01:59,440
But on the other hand?

33
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:03,520
Well, a very rigid enforcement of deportations drastically reduces

34
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:06,720
the expected utility and stability for those irregular migrants

35
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:10,000
who are not or cannot be deported.

36
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:11,920
If they know they could be kicked out tomorrow,

37
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:14,080
the incentive to integrate vanishes

38
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:17,920
and the famous opportunity cost we were talking about drops even further.

39
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:19,200
The big question is,

40
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:22,080
in reality, which of these two forces wins?

41
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:23,760
Let's move on to the second point.

42
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:27,840
The Italian quota system. To answer that big question,

43
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:31,760
our study uses a natural laboratory that is perfectly suited:

44
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:32,960
Italy.

45
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:37,280
Historically, Italian migration policy has relied on a system of annual quotas.

46
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:41,040
But the interesting thing is the total disconnection between what the law says

47
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:42,960
and what happens in practice.

48
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:45,760
In theory, an employer should hire a foreign worker

49
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:48,320
even before they set foot in the country.

50
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:49,359
But let's be honest.

51
00:02:49,359 --> 00:02:52,159
Who would ever hire someone without ever having seen them?

52
00:02:52,159 --> 00:02:54,000
So, the typical process is different.

53
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:55,680
They enter Italy unofficially,

54
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:58,959
perhaps simply by letting a completely normal tourist visa expire.

55
00:02:58,959 --> 00:03:00,319
Then they start working under the table,

56
00:03:00,319 --> 00:03:03,759
hoping one day to find an employer willing to sponsor them,

57
00:03:03,759 --> 00:03:06,639
right when the government quotas are opened.

58
00:03:06,639 --> 00:03:11,439
In this limbo, the risk on paper is immediate deportation,

59
00:03:11,439 --> 00:03:14,000
but the reality is much more complicated,

60
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,840
and physically deporting a person costs the state a lot.

61
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:22,479
In addition, those who are stopped very often do not have valid identity documents with them.

62
00:03:22,479 --> 00:03:25,840
And as if that weren't enough, countries of origin often

63
00:03:25,840 --> 00:02:29,680
oppose repatriation if there is no absolute certainty of nationality.

64
00:02:29,680 --> 00:03:32,879
The final result of this short circuit is a paradox.

65
00:03:32,879 --> 00:03:36,240
In the vast majority of cases, there is no actual return flight,

66
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:39,360
but a written injunction is simply delivered,

67
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:42,719
the famous expulsion order, which dictates leaving the country,

68
00:03:42,719 --> 00:03:45,920
a piece of paper that, in fact, remains unenforced.

69
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,680
And here we are, at the third point: the role of agreements.

70
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:53,360
There is indeed a vital exception to this rule of inefficiency,

71
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:57,600
and it is exactly the piece of the puzzle that allows the paper to work its magic.

72
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:01,040
The trick lies entirely in the bilateral agreements between Italy

73
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:05,360
and certain specific countries of origin, created to control undocumented flows.

74
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:09,600
Just think, the very first one was signed with Poland in 1994.

75
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:13,040
Looking at the data in the table extracted from the study, it is clear.

76
00:04:13,039 --> 00:04:14,959
If there is a bilateral agreement,

77
00:04:14,959 --> 00:04:19,519
the actual execution of deportation undergoes a statistically massive increase,

78
00:04:19,519 --> 00:04:21,599
even after adjusting the data for other variables,

79
00:04:21,599 --> 00:04:24,719
such as geographical distance or the volume of arrests.

80
00:04:24,719 --> 00:04:28,399
Let's put it in simpler terms, with some percentages:

81
00:04:28,399 --> 00:04:30,000
without an agreement between states,

82
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,639
on average, only 25% of stopped people are actually repatriated.

83
00:04:34,639 --> 00:04:38,800
But if the agreement exists, the percentage jumps to 41%.

84
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:41,680
This difference is pure gold for an economist,

85
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:44,800
because it creates two perfect natural groups to compare:

86
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:47,920
on one hand, irregular migrants with a low risk of deportation,

87
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:52,400
on the other hand, irregular migrants with a much higher and concrete risk of deportation.

88
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:54,400
Fourth fundamental step:

89
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:56,199
the "click day" experiment.

90
00:04:56,199 --> 00:04:57,639
Now that we have our two groups,

91
00:04:57,639 --> 00:05:02,759
how do we measure whether this high risk of deportation fosters or deters crime?

92
00:05:02,759 --> 00:05:05,960
It is the classic challenge for those who study these issues:

93
00:05:05,960 --> 00:05:07,759
isolating the variable.

94
00:05:07,759 --> 00:05:11,120
How can we assert with scientific certainty

95
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:15,199
that it is precisely the lack of legal status that pushes someone toward crime

96
00:05:15,199 --> 00:05:20,040
and not perhaps other differences in background, education, or social conditions?

97
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:21,840
A brilliant idea was needed.

98
00:05:21,840 --> 00:05:24,319
And Pinotti found that brilliant idea,

99
00:05:24,319 --> 00:05:28,240
by exploiting the notorious Italian "click day" mechanism.

100
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:29,720
Imagine the scene.

101
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:33,560
Residence permits, up for grabs through an online portal

102
00:05:33,560 --> 00:05:36,560
that opened its doors at exactly 8:00 AM.

103
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:40,480
An actual, instantaneous digital bottleneck was created.

104
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:43,759
Applications were processed by the servers to the millisecond

105
00:05:43,759 --> 00:05:46,920
in a strict order of arrival, from first to last,

106
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:51,400
stopping abruptly as soon as the annual quota set by the government was exhausted.

107
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:55,879
Pinotti examined the entire universe of male applicants in 2008,

108
00:05:55,879 --> 00:05:58,280
something like 110,000 applications,

109
00:05:58,280 --> 00:06:00,600
but the real stroke of genius was this.

110
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:03,480
He focused only on those who clicked "send"

111
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:07,480
in a very narrow one-hour window around the exact cutoff time,

112
00:06:07,480 --> 00:06:09,080
when the quotas ran out.

113
00:06:09,199 --> 00:06:12,079
In those hectic minutes, with slowed-down servers,

114
00:06:12,079 --> 00:06:17,519
managing to get in before or after the limit was literally a lottery, pure chance.

115
00:06:17,519 --> 00:06:21,479
You have two completely identical people clicking at almost the same instant:

116
00:06:21,479 --> 00:06:23,399
one becomes legal, the other does not.

117
00:06:23,399 --> 00:06:27,159
By anonymously cross-referencing this sample with criminal records,

118
00:06:27,159 --> 00:06:31,759
we obtain very clean data, entirely free of statistical distortions.

119
00:06:31,759 --> 00:06:33,680
Fifth item on the agenda.

120
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:35,680
Crime data.

121
00:06:35,959 --> 00:06:37,000
What emerged,

122
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,639
then, from this unrepeatable experiment?

123
00:06:39,639 --> 00:06:42,480
The very first data point is glaring:

124
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:45,840
irregular status in itself generates crime.

125
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:48,879
Receiving a rejection and remaining outside the quotas

126
00:06:48,879 --> 00:06:52,040
increases the probability of committing a serious crime

127
00:06:52,040 --> 00:06:55,280
in the following year by 0.6%.

128
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:57,280
It may seem like a little, but beware:

129
00:06:57,280 --> 00:07:01,560
this calculation is based on a baseline rate of just 0.9%.

130
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:04,720
In short, the numbers confirm the initial theory:

131
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:07,040
by shutting people out of legal pathways,

132
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:10,400
they are inevitably pushed toward illegal channels.

133
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:12,200
And here is the twist.

134
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:14,120
This increase is not the same for everyone.

135
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:15,760
Remember the two groups from earlier?

136
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:16,480
Well,

137
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:18,840
for those coming from countries with strong bilateral agreements,

138
00:07:18,840 --> 00:07:21,960
where the risk of being caught and put on a plane is real,

139
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:24,800
the probability of turning to crime due to irregular status

140
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:27,680
grows by only 0.3%.

141
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:29,360
On the other hand, for those coming from countries

142
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:30,400
without agreements, where

143
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:33,200
they know very well that deportations are just talk on paper,

144
00:07:33,199 --> 00:07:36,560
the increase in crime shoots up to 0.8%.

145
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:42,319
This result finally resolves the big economic dilemma we started with.

146
00:07:42,319 --> 00:07:43,759
What do the numbers tell us?

147
00:07:43,759 --> 00:07:46,759
They tell us that strictly enforcing deportations

148
00:07:46,759 --> 00:07:50,319
does not trigger a wave of crime caused by the desperation

149
00:07:50,319 --> 00:07:52,360
of those who slip through the cracks of justice.

150
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:53,279
On the contrary,

151
00:07:53,279 --> 00:07:56,279
the incapacitation effect wins hands down.

152
00:07:56,279 --> 00:07:58,919
Physically removing individuals from the territory

153
00:07:58,919 --> 00:08:00,560
prevents far more crimes

154
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:04,720
than the drop in opportunity cost for those who remain can generate.

155
00:08:04,720 --> 00:08:06,319
It is one of those revelations

156
00:08:06,319 --> 00:08:09,720
that completely change the lens through which we should judge

157
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:12,959
the real impact of laws in our society.

158
00:08:12,959 --> 00:08:14,840
Sixth and final point:

159
00:08:14,840 --> 00:08:16,840
conclusions for policy.

160
00:08:16,840 --> 00:08:19,439
Faced with such a clear empirical picture,

161
00:08:19,439 --> 00:08:21,240
what should governments do?

162
00:08:21,240 --> 00:08:25,079
The author leaves us with extremely pragmatic guidelines.

163
00:08:25,079 --> 00:08:30,040
The economist's suggestion does not favor any political faction.

164
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:31,600
It simply says this:

165
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:34,120
once the state decides on a quota system,

166
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:38,600
it must enforce it with extreme rigor, cost what it may.

167
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:40,320
But—and it's a huge "but"—

168
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:44,279
if the state realizes that enforcing these deportations is technically

169
00:08:44,279 --> 00:08:46,960
or economically impossible,

170
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:51,399
the only true rational way out is to proceed with regularizations.

171
00:08:51,399 --> 00:08:53,399
The fatal mistake, in both cases,

172
00:08:53,399 --> 00:08:54,639
is to turn a blind eye

173
00:08:54,639 --> 00:08:58,399
and leave thousands of people parked in a dead end.

174
00:08:58,439 --> 00:09:02,720
This idea is perfectly captured in this quote from the study:

175
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:06,319
"Governments and migration authorities should avoid the formation

176
00:09:06,319 --> 00:09:08,639
of large pools of unauthorized immigrants,

177
00:09:08,639 --> 00:09:11,399
with a low opportunity cost of committing crimes."

178
00:09:11,399 --> 00:09:13,799
At the end of the day, it is not a matter of ideology,

179
00:09:13,799 --> 00:09:17,519
it is a pure and simple matter of economic incentives and social stability.

180
00:09:17,519 --> 00:09:20,759
All this analysis brings us to a broader reflection:

181
00:09:20,759 --> 00:09:25,360
finding the balance between raw economic incentives and the laws of the state.

182
00:09:25,399 --> 00:09:28,800
This is the true great challenge for modern nations.

183
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:32,560
How can we balance, once and for all, these economic realities

184
00:09:32,560 --> 00:09:34,159
and collective security,

185
00:09:34,159 --> 00:09:37,960
knowing that gray areas only bring negative consequences?

186
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:41,320
It is a crucial question that this purely objective approach

187
00:09:41,320 --> 00:09:44,440
fortunately forces us to look straight in the face,

188
00:09:44,440 --> 00:09:46,000
well beyond the slogans.

189
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,840
We will stop here, but the invitation is open for you to delve deeper.

190
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:50,840
Thank you for following this analysis.